  – Drone_6 at (25,21): x distance = 12.
  – Drone_5 at (25,29): distance ≈ 12.2.
  – The others are farther away.
  Thus we choose Drone_2, Drone_6, then Drone_5 to protect Field_4.

5. We assign Drone_2, Drone_6, and Drone_5 to Field_4 (“protecting Field_4”).

6. The remaining drones are Drone_1, Drone_3, Drone_4, Drone_7, and Drone_8. Next we distribute these among the remaining fields in order of threat:
  – Field_2 is next (threat 0.10) and needs 4 drones. It already has Drone_4 protecting it (from its initial state), but needs 3 more. Looking at these remaining drones and considering their proximity to Field_2 (spanning x 6–22, y 30–45), we choose the ones that are closer in vertical distance:
   • Drone_3 (at 16,15) and Drone_7 (at 8,15) are nearer than those with y=7.
   • Between Drone_1 (16,7) and Drone_8 (8,7), we choose Drone_1 (they are tied, so we pick one arbitrarily).
  Thus, we assign Drone_1, Drone_3, and Drone_7 to Field_2.
  – The only one that remains, Drone_8, is then assigned to Field_1 (“protecting Field_1”). Field_1 already had 4 drones in its initial state but since lower-threat fields get lower priority regarding limited resources, only the leftover drone remains for Field_1.
  – Field_3 (threat 0.04) receives no drones.

The final groups are therefore as listed above.
