To explain briefly our reasoning:

1. We first sort the four fields by their current threat levels. Field_3 (0.12) is the most threatened, then Field_1 and Field_4 (both 0.09), and finally Field_2 (0.06).

2. For Field_3 the full‐protection requirement is 2 drones and it already has two drones (Drone_2 and Drone_3). So no extra help is needed there.

3. Next we sort the “remaining” drones by their distance to Field_3 (using its approximate center (39,40)); although the sorted order (Drone_1, Drone_8, Drone_5, Drone_4, Drone_6, Drone_7) would be used if extra drones were needed for Field_3, no extra allocation is required.

4. We then “distribute the remaining drones to the other fields” in order of decreasing threat. Both Field_1 and Field_4 (threat 0.09) are more important than Field_2. Originally Field_1 already has 3 drones (Drone_4, Drone_6, Drone_7) but needs 4 for full protection, while Field_4 has exactly the 3 it requires (Drone_1, Drone_5, Drone_8). With only eight drones available the best we can do is to leave Field_3 and Field_4 fully protected and have Field_1 partially protected (3 instead of 4) while Field_2 remains without any protection.

5. Thus the final assignment is exactly as shown above.

This allocation minimizes expected damage by ensuring that the highest–threat field (Field_3) and one of the equally threatened fields (Field_4) are fully covered, while the remaining drones are left with Field_1 (partial protection) and Field_2 (no drones) because of the limited resources.
